cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "TACIT: theory-anchored qualitative coding with researcher-held interpretive authority"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
version: 1.0.2
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/NYCU-FUN-TECH-LAB/TACIT"
authors:
  - family-names: Chang
    given-names: Hung-Chi
    affiliation: "Institute of Management of Technology, National Yang Ming Chiao Tung University"
    email: "chang.hung-chi.mg11@nycu.edu.tw"
abstract: >-
  A Streamlit application for theory-driven qualitative coding of interview
  transcripts. Coding frameworks are pluggable and can be constructed from
  literature retrieved via OpenAlex, with a guard that rejects citations
  absent from the retrieved corpus and per-dimension researcher approval.
  Model coding is treated as a draft: the model's original judgement is
  retained alongside every researcher revision, so the correction rate is
  reportable. Inter-rater reliability is computed over a sampling frame that
  contains unmarked units, so model recall is estimable, and statistics whose
  assumptions fail are withheld rather than printed. Language models may be
  served locally through Ollama or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so
  transcripts need not leave the researcher's machine.
keywords:
  - qualitative research
  - computer-assisted qualitative data analysis
  - deductive coding
  - thematic analysis
  - inter-rater reliability
  - large language models
  - local inference
  - research ethics
  - reproducibility
  - responsible innovation
